input.content:
# Implement review follow-ups for `test_rb_create`

## Context

A prior review of `test_rb_create` in [tests/test_rbtree.c](tests/test_rbtree.c) surfaced five gaps. The user asked to draft implementations of these changes. Two of the five needed a scope decision from the user first:

- **`rb_validate` check on an empty tree**: `rb_validate` is one of the five functions CLAUDE.md scopes into M1, and an empty tree trivially satisfies every RB invariant, but the test never calls it. The catch: `rb_validate` is still a stub returning `-1` ([src/rbtree.c:94-100](src/rbtree.c#L94-L100)), so adding `assert(rb_validate(t) == 0)` will turn `make test` red until `rb_validate` is implemented. **User decision: add it now anyway, deliberately, as a TDD marker** — this change will leave `make test` failing until a later commit implements `rb_validate`. That is expected, not a bug in this change.
- **Self-checking leak detection on the two fault-injection failure paths**: today only `make memcheck` catches a leak if `rb_create`'s cleanup path forgets to free the first allocation on the second `rb_malloc` failure. Making this self-checking under `make test` requires a free-call counter in [tests/fault_malloc.c](tests/fault_malloc.c), not just `test_rbtree.c`. **User decision: include `fault_malloc.c` in scope.**

The other three gaps (counting-free instrumentation for `value_free`, a comment/test-count mismatch, and a comment about magic-number coupling) are straightforward and scoped to `test_rbtree.c` alone.

Net effect of this change: it is test-only (plus the small `fault_malloc.c` counter), adds real regression coverage for `rb_validate`, `value_free` wiring, and leak-free unwind behavior, and is **expected to leave `make test` red** on the `rb_validate` assert until `rb_validate` is implemented in a later commit. Per CLAUDE.md, this change should not be committed until that follow-up lands and all three gates (`make test`, `make asan`, `make memcheck`) pass green.

## Changes

### 1. `tests/fault_malloc.c` — add a free-call counter

Add a counter that tracks how many times `rb_free` has been called since the last `fault_malloc_arm`, plus an accessor. This lets the test assert exactly how many frees happened during a faulted `rb_create` call, without depending on Valgrind.

```c
static int fault_armed = 0;
static int fault_target = 0;
static int fault_count = 0;
static int free_count = 0;                 /* NEW */

void fault_malloc_arm(int nth_call_to_fail)
{
    fault_target = nth_call_to_fail;
    fault_count = 0;
    free_count = 0;                         /* NEW: reset on each arm */
    fault_armed = 1;
}

void fault_malloc_disarm(void)
{
    fault_armed = 0;
}

int fault_malloc_free_count(void)           /* NEW */
{
    return free_count;
}

void *rb_malloc(size_t size)
{
    if (fault_armed) {
        fault_count++;
        if (fault_count == fault_target) {
            fault_armed = 0;
            return NULL;
        }
    }
    return malloc(size);
}

void rb_free(void *ptr)
{
    free_count++;                           /* NEW */
    free(ptr);
}
```

### 2. `tests/test_rbtree.c` — declare the new extern, add a counting free fn

Near the existing `fault_malloc_arm`/`fault_malloc_disarm` externs:

```c
extern void fault_malloc_arm(int nth_call_to_fail);
extern void fault_malloc_disarm(void);
extern int fault_malloc_free_count(void);      /* NEW */
```

